content.strength_and_weaknesses:
The paper overall considers a simple but interesting setup in which domain shift is reduced by simply cropping out the parts of the image which are the most domain specific (human and robotic hands). This setup is interesting because it can be easily applied in many contexts. 

Despite the interestingness and appeal of the considered setup, I found the experimental validation limited. In particular:
 - The paper states that egocentric and third person human demonstration provide suboptimal data. However, this statement is never assessed experimentally. I would have expected to see experiments comparing third person data vs egocentric data vs hand-centric data vs cropped hand-centric data. In the lack of these experiments it's hard to verify these claims. This is relevant as collecting third person video or even egocentric videos is much more common than collecting hand-centric video, so data may already be available in some contexts;
 - The proposed approach is not compared with respect to any prior work tackling human to robot policy transfer. I am referring in particular to the works mentioned in Section "2 Related Work", e.g., (Liu et al., 2018; Sharma et al., 2019), (Smith et al., 2019; Li et al., 2021; Xiong et al., 2021), or even just CycleGAN (Zhu et al., 2017). This line of approach has attempted to reduce the domain shift between robotic and human data and hence they could in principle be applied in the considered contexts instead of simply zeroing out the upper part of the images. Without these experiments, it is not clear what is the real advantage of the proposed setup over existing techniques;
 -  Even if the proposed approach is learning from human demonstrations, these are still performed in constrained settings in a lab with similar visual appearance. Indeed, the actual variations between environment changes do not always reflect realistic conditions (e.g., see Figure 4). I wonder how the proposed approach compares with respect to recent work on human-robot imitation in the wild (e.g., https://human2robot.github.io/)
